    "status": "complete",
    "review": "The risk score of 4.5 out of 10.0 is reasonable under Guardia's conservative static scoring framework, though it reflects the limits of static inspection rather than benign behavior. The score is almost entirely driven by the unsigned binary baseline (4.0 points) and minor entry-point CPU instruction heuristics (0.5 points). The outer PE acts primarily as a loader, containing a 12.8 MB overlay that bundles a full Python runtime and an obfuscated script.\n\nA likely false positive is the CPU instruction finding. The presence of cpuid and interrupt instructions at the entry point is typical for compiler runtime initialization and CRT feature detection rather than intentional evasion. Similarly, the isolated IsDebuggerPresent API in standard runtime imports is common across benign commercial packaging.\n\nA notable false negative is likely present regarding the obfuscated overlay payload. The archive member app/pyobfuscate_com.py utilizes commercial obfuscation (pyobfuscate.com), dynamic runtime reflection, and string references to anti-analysis tooling including gdb, frida, and pyrasite. Because bounded script deobfuscation resolved fewer than half of the expressions and native extension modules remained uninspected, malicious network, credential access, or execution capabilities may be completely concealed behind the obfuscation layer.\n\nRecommended next steps include executing the binary in an instrumented sandbox equipped to handle anti-debugging and anti-tracing techniques. Analysts should extract and manually deobfuscate app/pyobfuscate_com.py to uncover its unresolved dynamic imports and secondary staging logic. Finally, inspect the contents of the nested python314.zip archive and correlate the recovered SHA-256 hash against threat intelligence repositories.",
